L0@P0-1: Working with GIS LayersCopyright © 2020 Esri. All rights reserved. https://www.esri.com/
L1@P1: Layers are the contents of a map. They include a wide range of information about people, the earth, life, and so on, and are composed of imagery, tiles, features, and more.
L2@P1: [continuation included in the complete reading; see exact page text below]
L3@P1: Your company is introducing online GIS into its suite of tools that beginning employees must know to compete in today’s workplace. They have asked you to create an online lesson that introduces employees to GIS. One of the main focuses that they want shown in the lesson is the wide variety of data types that can be represented in a GIS.
L4@P1: Builds skills in understanding scale; accuracy and resolution; accessing tables; interpreting legends; distinguishing layer types; adding x,y (longitude and latitude) data; adding address data (geocoding).
L5@P1: Software requirements: ArcGIS Online account. Estimated time: under 30 minutes.
L6@P1-2: Lesson Overview.
L20@P2: Step 1: Turning layers on and off. The map's layers are listed and can be turned on and off.
L31@P2: Step 2: Resolution, accuracy, and scale.
L32@P2: Accuracy tells how closely image matches true ground value; resolution is the finest detail displayed.
L35@P2: Scale bars visually indicate feature size and distance. A scale bar is a line/bar divided into parts representing ground length, usually multiples of map units. Large-scale maps have large fractions; small-scale maps have small fractions. A town map might be 1:10,000; a world map 1:100,000,000.
L41@P2: The exercise uses the World Imagery layer.
L44@P3: Different imagery resolutions appear as users zoom. 30-centimeter resolution contains more detail than 15-meter resolution. Metadata identifies provider, acquisition date, resolution and accuracy.
L55@P4: Step 3: Observing raster data.
L56@P4: In its simplest form, a raster consists of a matrix of cells (or pixels) organized into rows and columns (or a grid) where each cell contains a value representing information, such as elevation, temperature, or land cover.
L57@P4: World Imagery is an example of a raster image. A digital model is used in which each pixel represents an elevation value in meters.
L60@P4: The exercise turns on the World Elevation GMTED layer and zooms to individual pixels.
L65@P4: Step 4: Examine attributes and legends of vector layers.
L66@P4: Example layers: World Countries, World Cities, World Rivers.
L67@P4: These are examples of vector layers. Each layer has a spatial component (polygon, point, line) that is dynamically linked to a table of attributes. Each layer has its own unique table of attributes that can be accessed by clicking Show Table.
L70@P4: The exercise opens the attribute tables for the vector layers.
L72@P4: Students list each vector type (polygon, point, line) and two attributes from the tables.
L81@P4-5: Step 5: Enter x,y data (longitude and latitude).
L82@P5: One can add point data to a GIS map in several ways, including using specific longitude and latitude coordinates.
L85@P5: Coordinate values are decimal degrees.
L103@P5: Step 6: Enter address data (geocoding).
L104@P5: Geocoding is the process of turning an address into a locational point on a map.
L113@P6: Assessment reiterates geocoding and asks users to build maps with vector and raster layers and locations found by coordinates/address.
L133@P7: Exercise answers.
L136@P7: Answer key asks vector type and attributes for layers.
L140@P7: World Countries — Polygon; World Cities — Point; World Rivers — Line.
L150@P7-8: Exercise Answers; remaining pages contain address, copyright, author and support matter.
L151@P8: Esri address and copyright.
L162@P9: About the author; lesson written by a K-12 educator with Learn ArcGIS support.

